IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `fsc-dates`

-- Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) -- the provisions a CLAIMANT meets: the retail client who got bad financial advice, the
-- investor misled or kept in the dark, the person who bought a financial product and wants out, the superannuation member
-- or dependant complaining to AFCA, and the whistleblower who suffers for a disclosure.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who brings, or may bring, the claim. Every question is phrased as that person would ask it ("Can
-- I ...?", "By when must I ...?", "Who must pay me?"), on facts that person could state. Licensees, AFCA, ASIC, the court
-- and the death benefit decision-maker get no seat; their duties appear as what the person may claim or must be given.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED. Liability of licensees for their representatives, Part 7.6 Division 6 (ss 917A, 917B, 917C,
-- 917D, 917E, 917F(1), (2)). The best interests obligations, Part 7.7A Division 2 (ss 961, 961B, 961F, 961G, 961H, 961J,
-- 961M, 961P), with s 960A. Cooling-off, Part 7.9 Division 5 (ss 1019A, 1019B). Misleading or deceptive conduct and its
-- civil action (ss 1041H, 1041I). Continuous disclosure with fault, s 674A(2), (3), (4), with compensation orders (ss
-- 1317HA, 1317K and the s 1317E(3) table), and the s 1317HA order for every other financial services civil penalty
-- provision in the table (contravention asked), with the insider trading amounts of s 1043L(3), (4). AFCA, Part 7.10A: s
-- 1051(2)(d), (4)(e), (f) and the superannuation complaint provisions of Division 3 (ss 1053, 1053A, 1053B, 1055, 1056,
-- 1056A, 1057). Whistleblowers, Part 9.4AAA (ss 1317AA, 1317AAA,
-- 1317AAB, 1317AAC, 1317AAD, 1317AADA, 1317AAE, 1317AB, 1317AD, 1317ADA, 1317AE, 1317AH).
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 149, compilation date 19 September 2026, includes amendments to Act No. 87, 2026 (register id
-- C2026C00432), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A00818.txt. The Corporations Regulations 2001 are NOT
-- deposited: what they prescribe (more whistleblowers, recipients and entities; cooling-off exclusions; s 917C(3)(ba)
-- products; professional standards schemes) is asked as a fact.
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation at 2026-10-13: "Corporations Act 2001", Commonwealth of Australia,
-- licensed under CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Corporations Act 2001 -- the claimant's questions`

GIVEN b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`as an answer` b MEANS IF b THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Part 7.6 Division 6 -- is the licensee responsible for what its representative did`

-- s 917C sorts the licensees of a representative who acts for more than one. (2): "the representative of one of the
-- licensees only in respect of a particular class of financial service" -- that licensee; (3): the representative of
-- more than one of them for that class, the conduct relating to a kind of product prescribed for (3)(ba), and the conduct
-- within authority for one licensee (d) or for two or more (e); (4): "In any other case, all of the licensees are jointly
-- and severally responsible". s 917A(3) treats the conduct as within authority for each licensee that issued or
-- transferred a product of the prescribed kind as a result of it.
DECLARE `Licensee responsibility` IS ONE OF
    `Division 6 does not reach this conduct`
    `the licensee is responsible (s 917B)`
    `the one licensee authorised for that class of service is responsible (s 917C(2))`
    `the authorising licensee is responsible (s 917C(3)(d))`
    `the authorising licensees are jointly and severally responsible (s 917C(3)(e))`
    `all the licensees are jointly and severally responsible (s 917C(4))`
    `no licensee is responsible: the lack of authority was disclosed to me (s 917D)`

GIVEN `licensees authorised for the class` IS A NUMBER
      `prescribed kind` IS A BOOLEAN
      `licensees within authority` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Licensee responsibility`
`the s 917C sorting` `licensees authorised for the class` `prescribed kind` `licensees within authority` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `licensees authorised for the class` EQUALS 1 THEN `the one licensee authorised for that class of service is responsible (s 917C(2))`
           IF `licensees authorised for the class` AT LEAST 2 AND `prescribed kind` AND `licensees within authority` EQUALS 1 THEN `the authorising licensee is responsible (s 917C(3)(d))`
           IF `licensees authorised for the class` AT LEAST 2 AND `prescribed kind` AND `licensees within authority` AT LEAST 2 THEN `the authorising licensees are jointly and severally responsible (s 917C(3)(e))`
           OTHERWISE `all the licensees are jointly and severally responsible (s 917C(4))`

-- s 917A(1): Division 6 "applies to any conduct of a representative ... (a) that relates to the provision of a financial
-- service; and (b) on which a third person (the client) could reasonably be expected to rely; and (c) on which the client
-- in fact relied in good faith". s 917B: one licensee "is responsible ... whether or not the representative's conduct is
-- within authority". s 917D: not responsible if the conduct "is not within authority" for any licensee and the
-- representative disclosed that before the client relied, with the clarity and prominence a person would reasonably
-- require. s 917E: the responsibility extends to "any loss or damage suffered by the client". s 917F(1), (2): the client
-- has "the same remedies against the licensee" as against the representative, all jointly and severally liable.
-- Outside s 917A(1) the Division is silent, not a no: the answer is the first constructor, which the NOTES read as
-- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 917A(1)).
@export Is a financial services licensee responsible to me for what its representative (an adviser, employee or authorised representative) did, so that I can claim my loss from the licensee as well as the representative (Corporations Act 2001 Part 7.6 Division 6, ss 917A to 917F)?
GIVEN `did it relate to a financial service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative's conduct related to providing a financial service (for example financial product advice, dealing in a product, arranging insurance)
      `could I reasonably be expected to rely on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a client in your position could reasonably be expected to rely on the conduct
      `did I rely on it in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you in fact relied on the conduct in good faith
      `how many licensees does the representative act for` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Australian financial services licensees the person was a representative of at the time
      `how many of them authorised the representative for this class of service` IS A NUMBER @desc Of those licensees, how many the person represented for the class of financial service the conduct relates to (for example personal advice on life insurance)
      `was it a prescribed kind of product` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct related to a kind of financial product prescribed by the Corporations Regulations for s 917C(3)(ba) (answer no if you do not know)
      `for how many licensees was it within authority` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many licensees the conduct was within authority: within the scope of the representative's employment, director's duties or the authority the licensee gave; count a licensee that issued or transferred the product as a result
      `was the lack of authority disclosed to me first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before you relied on the conduct, the representative told you that it was not within the authority of the licensee (or licensees), clearly and prominently enough for deciding whether to acquire the service
GIVETH A `Licensee responsibility`
`Is the licensee responsible to me for its representative` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`did it relate to a financial service` AND `could I reasonably be expected to rely on it` AND `did I rely on it in good faith`)) THEN `Division 6 does not reach this conduct`
           IF `for how many licensees was it within authority` EQUALS 0 AND `was the lack of authority disclosed to me first` THEN `no licensee is responsible: the lack of authority was disclosed to me (s 917D)`
           IF `how many licensees does the representative act for` AT MOST 1 THEN `the licensee is responsible (s 917B)`
           OTHERWISE `the s 917C sorting` `how many of them authorised the representative for this class of service` `was it a prescribed kind of product` `for how many licensees was it within authority`

§§ `Part 7.7A Division 2 -- did my adviser act in my best interests`

-- s 961(1): the Division "applies in relation to the provision of personal advice ... to a person (the client) as a
-- retail client". Outside it the Division is silent: NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 961(1)). Whether advice is
-- "personal advice" (s 766B(3)) and whether the person is a "retail client" (s 761G) are long definitions of the Act that
-- this encoding does not work out; they are asked (departure D1 in NOTES.md).
DECLARE `Advice subject` IS ONE OF
    `a general insurance product`
    `a basic banking product`
    `basic banking and general insurance products`
    `consumer credit insurance`
    `another financial product or subject`

-- s 961B(2): "The provider satisfies the duty in subsection (1), if the provider proves" (a) to (g). (3): an agent or
-- employee of an Australian ADI advising on a subject that "relates only to" a basic banking product, a general insurance
-- product, consumer credit insurance or a combination "satisfies the duty ... in relation to the advice given in relation
-- to the basic banking product and the general insurance product if the provider takes the steps mentioned in paragraphs
-- (2)(a), (b) and (c)". (4): "To the extent that the subject matter of the advice sought by the client is a general
-- insurance product", steps (a) to (c) suffice. s 961F: basic banking product = a basic deposit product, a non-cash
-- payment facility, traveller's cheques, or a prescribed product. Steps (a) to (c) are identifying the client's
-- objectives, situation and needs and the subject matter, and inquiring where information was reasonably apparently
-- incomplete; (d) to (g) are the expertise check, a reasonable investigation of products where one would be recommended,
-- basing every judgement on the client's relevant circumstances, and any other step reasonably in the client's best
-- interests. Proof by the provider is an input. Not proving the steps does not establish a breach: s 961B(2) is an
-- "if", and the general duty in (1) is left to the court (fork F3).
DECLARE `Best interests answer` IS ONE OF
    `Division 2 does not apply to this advice`
    `the duty is satisfied: the adviser proves every s 961B(2) step`
    `the duty is satisfied: the adviser proves the s 961B(2)(a) to (c) steps, which suffice for this advice`
    `not shown to be satisfied: whether the adviser acted in my best interests is for the court under s 961B(1)`

GIVEN `the subject` IS AN `Advice subject`, `an ADI's adviser` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first three steps suffice for` `the subject` `when given by` `an ADI's adviser` IF
        `the subject` EQUALS `a general insurance product`
    OR  (    `an ADI's adviser`
         AND (   `the subject` EQUALS `a basic banking product`
              OR `the subject` EQUALS `basic banking and general insurance products`))

@export Did my financial adviser meet the duty to act in my best interests when giving me personal advice (Corporations Act 2001 s 961B)? The adviser satisfies it by proving each step in s 961B(2); for general insurance advice, and for a bank's adviser on basic banking or general insurance products, the first three steps are enough.
GIVEN `was it personal advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advice took into account (or a reasonable person would expect it to take into account) your objectives, financial situation or needs: personal advice, not general advice
      `was I a retail client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you received the advice as a retail client (most individuals are; a wholesale client is, for example, someone who meets the s 761G asset or income tests or buys a product costing $500,000 or more)
      `what was the advice about` IS AN `Advice subject` @desc What the advice you sought was about: only a general insurance product (such as home, car or travel insurance), only a basic banking product (a basic deposit account, a non-cash payment facility, traveller's cheques), only basic banking and general insurance products, consumer credit insurance, or another financial product or subject (such as superannuation, investments, life insurance)
      `was the adviser acting for a bank or other ADI` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adviser was an agent or employee of an Australian bank, building society or credit union (an ADI), or acted under its name by arrangement with it
      `does the adviser prove the first three steps` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adviser can prove that they identified the objectives, financial situation and needs you disclosed, identified the subject of the advice and your relevant circumstances, and made reasonable inquiries where your information was apparently incomplete or inaccurate (s 961B(2)(a) to (c))
      `does the adviser prove the other steps` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adviser can also prove that they checked they had the expertise (declining if not), reasonably investigated and assessed products where recommending one would be reasonable, based all judgements on your relevant circumstances, and took any other step reasonably in your best interests (s 961B(2)(d) to (g))
GIVETH A `Best interests answer`
`Did my adviser meet the best interests duty` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`was it personal advice` AND `was I a retail client`)) THEN `Division 2 does not apply to this advice`
           IF `does the adviser prove the first three steps` AND `does the adviser prove the other steps` THEN `the duty is satisfied: the adviser proves every s 961B(2) step`
           IF `does the adviser prove the first three steps` AND `the first three steps suffice for` `what was the advice about` `when given by` `was the adviser acting for a bank or other ADI` THEN `the duty is satisfied: the adviser proves the s 961B(2)(a) to (c) steps, which suffice for this advice`
           OTHERWISE `not shown to be satisfied: whether the adviser acted in my best interests is for the court under s 961B(1)`

-- s 961G: "The provider must only provide the advice to the client if it would be reasonable to conclude that the advice
-- is appropriate to the client, had the provider satisfied the duty under section 961B". The advice was given, so the
-- duty is broken exactly when that conclusion would not be reasonable: an "only if", JUST FALSE where it would be.
@export Did my adviser break the rule that advice may only be given if it would be reasonable to conclude it is appropriate to me (Corporations Act 2001 s 961G)?
GIVEN `was it personal advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advice took into account (or a reasonable person would expect it to take into account) your objectives, financial situation or needs
      `was I a retail client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you received the advice as a retail client
      `would it be reasonable to conclude it was appropriate to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it would be reasonable to conclude that the advice was appropriate to you, had the adviser properly acted in your best interests (a judgment for the court; answer as the court finds or as you claim)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did my adviser breach s 961G` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`was it personal advice` AND `was I a retail client`))
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 961(1))
    ELSE `as an answer` (NOT `would it be reasonable to conclude it was appropriate to me`)

-- s 961H(1): "If it is reasonably apparent that information ... on which the advice is based is incomplete or
-- inaccurate, the provider must ... warn the client" that the advice may be based on incomplete or inaccurate information
-- and that the client should consider its appropriateness. (2): "at the same time as the advice is provided and ... by
-- the same means"; (3) the warning may be in the Statement of Advice; (4) where two or more give the advice, one
-- warning covers the others. Where nothing was reasonably apparent the duty does not arise: JUST FALSE (fork F4).
@export Did my adviser break the duty to warn me that the advice was based on incomplete or inaccurate information about me (Corporations Act 2001 s 961H)?
GIVEN `was it personal advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advice took into account (or a reasonable person would expect it to take into account) your objectives, financial situation or needs
      `was I a retail client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you received the advice as a retail client
      `was the information apparently incomplete or inaccurate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it would have been apparent to a person with reasonable expertise in the subject, taking care and assessing what you told the adviser, that the information about your objectives, financial situation and needs was incomplete or inaccurate (s 961C)
      `was I warned` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were warned that the advice is, or may be, based on incomplete or inaccurate information about you and that you should consider whether it is appropriate before acting on it (by your adviser or by another adviser who gave the advice with them)
      `was the warning given with the advice, by the same means or in the Statement of Advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the warning came at the same time as the advice, and by the same means (for example in the same meeting or document) or in the Statement of Advice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did my adviser breach s 961H` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`was it personal advice` AND `was I a retail client`)) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 961(1))
           IF (NOT `was the information apparently incomplete or inaccurate`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE `as an answer` (NOT (`was I warned` AND `was the warning given with the advice, by the same means or in the Statement of Advice`))

-- s 961J(1): "If the provider knows, or reasonably ought to know, that there is a conflict between the interests of the
-- client and the interests of" the provider, the licensee, an authorising authorised representative or an associate of
-- any of them, "the provider must give priority to the client's interests when giving the advice". (2): for an ADI's
-- adviser on a subject relating only to basic banking, general insurance, consumer credit insurance or a combination,
-- "subsection (1) does not apply to the extent that the advice relates to a basic banking product or a general insurance
-- product or a combination of those 2 products"; (3): nor "to the extent that the subject matter of the advice sought by
-- the client is a general insurance product". Flat negatives: JUST FALSE. No conflict known: the duty does not arise,
-- JUST FALSE (fork F4).
@export Did my adviser break the duty to put my interests first where they conflicted with the adviser's, the licensee's or an associate's (Corporations Act 2001 s 961J)?
GIVEN `was it personal advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advice took into account (or a reasonable person would expect it to take into account) your objectives, financial situation or needs
      `was I a retail client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you received the advice as a retail client
      `what was the advice about` IS AN `Advice subject` @desc What the advice you sought was about (general insurance, basic banking, both, consumer credit insurance, or another product or subject)
      `was the adviser acting for a bank or other ADI` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adviser was an agent or employee of an Australian bank, building society or credit union, or acted under its name by arrangement with it
      `did the adviser know or ought to know of a conflict` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adviser knew, or reasonably ought to have known, of a conflict between your interests and those of the adviser, the adviser's licensee, the authorised representative who authorised them, or an associate of any of them (for example a commission for recommending a product)
      `did the adviser give priority to my interests` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adviser gave priority to your interests when giving the advice (a judgment for the court; answer as the court finds or as you claim)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did my adviser breach s 961J` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`was it personal advice` AND `was I a retail client`)) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 961(1))
           IF `what was the advice about` EQUALS `a general insurance product` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `was the adviser acting for a bank or other ADI` AND (`what was the advice about` EQUALS `a basic banking product` OR `what was the advice about` EQUALS `basic banking and general insurance products`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `did the adviser know or ought to know of a conflict`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE `as an answer` (NOT `did the adviser give priority to my interests`)

-- s 961M(1): "This section applies if the client suffers loss or damage because of a contravention of a provision of
-- this Division." (2): the Court may order compensation by "(a) if the person who contravenes the provision is a
-- financial services licensee--that licensee; (b) if the person who contravenes the provision is a representative of a
-- financial services licensee, or 2 or more financial services licensees--the, or a, responsible licensee". s 961P: the
-- responsible licensee is the only licensee, or the licensee or licensees responsible "under the rules in section
-- 917C". Unlike s 953B(3A), s 961P does not switch off s 917D, and does not require the s 917A(1) reliance; the s 917C
-- sorting alone is applied (fork F5). The order runs only against licensees, never the individual adviser; s 961M(4) lets
-- the Court include the contravener's profits; s 961N lets it declare the contract void and order money returned.
DECLARE `Who can be ordered to compensate me` IS ONE OF
    `no order under s 961M on these facts`
    `the licensee that gave the advice (s 961M(2)(a))`
    `the one licensee the adviser represents (s 961P(a))`
    `the licensee responsible under the s 917C rules (s 961P(b)(i))`
    `each licensee jointly and severally responsible under the s 917C rules (s 961P(b)(ii))`

@export If my adviser broke a best interests duty (ss 961B, 961G, 961H or 961J) and I lost money because of it, which licensee can the court order to compensate me (Corporations Act 2001 ss 961M and 961P)?
GIVEN `was a Division 2 duty broken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adviser contravened a provision of Part 7.7A Division 2 (the answer to the s 961B, 961G, 961H or 961J questions, or as a court finds)
      `did I suffer loss because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered loss or damage because of that contravention
      `was the adviser itself a licensee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who gave the advice and broke the duty was itself an Australian financial services licensee (answer no if they were an employee or authorised representative of a licensee)
      `how many licensees does the adviser represent` IS A NUMBER @desc How many financial services licensees the adviser was a representative of at the time
      `how many of them authorised the adviser for this class of service` IS A NUMBER @desc Of those licensees, how many the adviser represented for the class of financial service the advice concerned
      `was it a prescribed kind of product` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advice related to a kind of financial product prescribed by the Corporations Regulations for s 917C(3)(ba) (answer no if you do not know)
      `for how many licensees was it within authority` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many licensees the advice was within the authority they gave (or within the adviser's employment)
GIVETH A `Who can be ordered to compensate me`
`Which licensee can be ordered to compensate me under s 961M` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`was a Division 2 duty broken` AND `did I suffer loss because of it`)) THEN `no order under s 961M on these facts`
           IF `was the adviser itself a licensee` THEN `the licensee that gave the advice (s 961M(2)(a))`
           IF `how many licensees does the adviser represent` AT MOST 1 THEN `the one licensee the adviser represents (s 961P(a))`
           IF `the s 917C sorting` `how many of them authorised the adviser for this class of service` `was it a prescribed kind of product` `for how many licensees was it within authority` EQUALS `the authorising licensees are jointly and severally responsible (s 917C(3)(e))` THEN `each licensee jointly and severally responsible under the s 917C rules (s 961P(b)(ii))`
           IF `the s 917C sorting` `how many of them authorised the adviser for this class of service` `was it a prescribed kind of product` `for how many licensees was it within authority` EQUALS `all the licensees are jointly and severally responsible (s 917C(4))` THEN `each licensee jointly and severally responsible under the s 917C rules (s 961P(b)(ii))`
           OTHERWISE `the licensee responsible under the s 917C rules (s 961P(b)(i))`

-- s 961M(6): "An action to recover the amount of the loss or damage may be begun at any time within 6 years after the
-- contravention."
@export By what day must I start court action to recover my loss from bad financial advice (Corporations Act 2001 s 961M(6))? Within 6 years after the contravention.
GIVEN `the day of the contravention` IS A DATE @desc The day the adviser broke the duty (usually the day the advice was given), ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-15
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I sue under s 961M` MEANS `the last day of 6 years after` `the day of the contravention`

§§ `Part 7.9 Division 5 -- cooling-off: can I return the product`

DECLARE `Product class` IS ONE OF
    `a risk insurance product`
    `an investment life insurance product`
    `a managed investment product`
    `a foreign passport fund product`
    `a superannuation product`
    `an RSA`
    `another kind of financial product`

DECLARE `How I acquired the product` IS ONE OF
    `it was issued to me`
    `it was sold to me under an offer that s 1012C applies to`
    `some other way`

-- s 1019B(3): "The right to return the product can only be exercised during the period of 14 days starting on the
-- earlier of: (a) the time when the confirmation requirement (if applicable) is complied with; or (b) the end of the
-- fifth business day after the day on which the product was issued or sold to the client." The period starts during a
-- day and runs 14 days; the encoding counts the whole of the 14th day after the start day (fork F8). Business days skip
-- weekends and the public or bank holidays the person counts (fork F9).
GIVEN `the day it was issued or sold to me` IS A DATE
      `have I received confirmation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I received confirmation` IS A DATE
      `weekday holidays in the first week` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the cooling-off period` `the day it was issued or sold to me` `have I received confirmation` `the day I received confirmation` `weekday holidays in the first week` MEANS
    `the date` 14 `days after` `the start day`
    WHERE
        `the fifth business day` MEANS `the weekday` (5 PLUS `weekday holidays in the first week`) `after` `the day it was issued or sold to me`
        `the start day` MEANS
            IF `have I received confirmation`
            THEN `the earlier of` `the day I received confirmation` `and` `the fifth business day`
            ELSE `the fifth business day`

@export What is the last day on which I can return my insurance, managed investment, superannuation or RSA product and get my money back under the 14-day cooling-off period (Corporations Act 2001 s 1019B(3))? The 14 days start when you receive confirmation of the transaction, or at the end of the fifth business day after the product was issued or sold to you, whichever is earlier.
GIVEN `the day it was issued or sold to me` IS A DATE @desc The day the product was issued or sold to you, ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-02
      `have I received confirmation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received confirmation of the transaction (or it has been made available to you through a facility such as an online account)
      `the day I received confirmation` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the confirmation or it became available to you (any date if you have not received it)
      `weekday holidays in the first week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public or bank holidays where you are fell on weekdays in the days after the issue or sale, up to the fifth business day (usually 0)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day of my cooling-off period` MEANS
    `the last day of the cooling-off period` `the day it was issued or sold to me` `have I received confirmation` `the day I received confirmation` `weekday holidays in the first week`

-- s 1019A(1): "this Division applies if" (a) a product of the listed classes "is provided in this jurisdiction to a
-- person as a retail client"
-- and (b) it is provided by issue or by sale under an offer s 1012C applies to; (2) the regulations may exclude. s
-- 1019B(1): "the client has the right to return the financial product ... and to have the money they paid to acquire the
-- product repaid. This is so even if the responsible person is being wound up." (2): by notifying "(a) in writing; or
-- (b) electronically; or (c) in any other way specified in the regulations". (5): not after the client has "exercised a
-- right or power that they have under the terms applicable to the product" (the Note: after a claim on an insurance
-- contract) or after the client's rights under the terms end. Each condition is the text's own "if", "only" or "cannot":
-- JUST FALSE where it fails.
@export Can I return my financial product and get back the money I paid, under the cooling-off period (Corporations Act 2001 ss 1019A and 1019B)? It covers risk insurance, investment life insurance, managed investment, foreign passport fund, superannuation and RSA products issued or sold to a retail client, for 14 days, unless you have used a right under the product (such as making an insurance claim) or your rights under it have ended.
GIVEN `what kind of product is it` IS A `Product class` @desc The kind of financial product: risk insurance (such as life, income protection or general insurance), investment life insurance, an interest in a managed investment scheme, a foreign passport fund product, a superannuation interest, a retirement savings account (RSA), or another kind
      `did I acquire it as a retail client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the product was provided to you as a retail client
      `was it provided to me in this jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the product was provided to you in Australia (in this jurisdiction)
      `how did I acquire it` IS A `How I acquired the product` @desc Whether it was issued to you, sold to you under an offer that s 1012C applies to (such as an offer for sale within 12 months of issue), or acquired some other way (for example bought on the stock market)
      `do the regulations exclude it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Corporations Regulations exclude this product or these circumstances from the cooling-off rules (answer no if you do not know)
      `the day it was issued or sold to me` IS A DATE @desc The day the product was issued or sold to you, ISO 8601
      `have I received confirmation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received confirmation of the transaction (or it was made available to you)
      `the day I received confirmation` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the confirmation (any date if you have not)
      `weekday holidays in the first week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public or bank holidays fell on weekdays after the issue or sale, up to the fifth business day (usually 0)
      `have I used a right under the product` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have exercised a right or power under the product's terms, for example made a claim on the insurance
      `have my rights under the product ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your rights under the product's terms have already ended (for example one week's travel cover that has run out)
      `the day I notify the issuer` IS A DATE @desc The day you notify (or will notify) the issuer or seller that you are returning the product, ISO 8601
      `do I notify in writing or electronically` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you notify in writing or electronically (for example by letter or email)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I return the product under the cooling-off period` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `what kind of product is it` EQUALS `another kind of financial product` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `did I acquire it as a retail client`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `was it provided to me in this jurisdiction`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `how did I acquire it` EQUALS `some other way` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `do the regulations exclude it` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `have I used a right under the product` OR `have my rights under the product ended` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `do I notify in writing or electronically`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE `as an answer` (`the day I notify the issuer` `is on or before` (`the last day of the cooling-off period` `the day it was issued or sold to me` `have I received confirmation` `the day I received confirmation` `weekday holidays in the first week`))

§§ `ss 1041H and 1041I -- misleading or deceptive conduct`

-- s 1041H(1): "A person must not, in this jurisdiction, engage in conduct, in relation to a financial product or a
-- financial service, that is misleading or deceptive or is likely to mislead or deceive." (3): conduct that contravenes s
-- 670A, 728, 738Y, 1021NA or 1021NB, or relates to a disclosure document or statement within s 953A or s 1022A, "does not
-- contravene subsection (1)". (4): a disclosing entity's conduct that does not contravene s 674A(2) (or s 675A(2)) but
-- would if the fault paragraph read like s 674(2)(d) "does not contravene subsection (1)". s 1041I(1): "A person who
-- suffers loss or damage by conduct of another person that was engaged in in contravention of section ... 1041H may
-- recover the amount of the loss or damage by action against that other person or against any person involved in the
-- contravention". Outside "in this jurisdiction" or "in relation to a financial product or a financial service" the
-- section is silent (the Australian Consumer Law may apply): NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 1041H(1)).
@export Can I recover my loss from someone whose misleading or deceptive conduct about a financial product or service caused it, by suing them or anyone involved (Corporations Act 2001 ss 1041H and 1041I)?
GIVEN `was it about a financial product or service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was in relation to a financial product (shares, managed fund interests, superannuation, insurance, deposits and so on) or a financial service (advice, dealing, arranging)
      `was it in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was engaged in in this jurisdiction (Australia)
      `was it misleading or deceptive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was misleading or deceptive, or likely to mislead or deceive (a judgment for the court; answer as the court finds or as you claim)
      `was it in a prospectus, PDS, FSG, SOA or takeover document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct is covered by the Act's own disclosure-document rules instead: a misleading takeover document (s 670A), fundraising document (s 728), CSF offer document (s 738Y), s 1021NA or 1021NB, a Financial Services Guide or Statement of Advice (s 953A), or a Product Disclosure Statement (s 1022A)
      `was it a listed entity's failure to disclose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct is a disclosing entity's failure to tell the market information under the continuous disclosure rules
      `did the entity know or was it reckless or negligent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was a failure to disclose: whether the entity knew, or was reckless or negligent about whether, the information would have a material effect on the price or value of its securities
      `did I suffer loss by it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered loss or damage by that conduct
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I recover my loss for misleading or deceptive conduct` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`was it about a financial product or service` AND `was it in Australia`)) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 1041H(1))
           IF `was it in a prospectus, PDS, FSG, SOA or takeover document` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `was it a listed entity's failure to disclose` AND (NOT `did the entity know or was it reckless or negligent`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE `as an answer` (`was it misleading or deceptive` AND `did I suffer loss by it`)

-- s 1041I(1B): for a claim for economic loss or damage to property under s 1041H, where the claimant suffered it "as a
-- result partly of the claimant's failure to take reasonable care" and partly of the conduct, and the defendant "did not
-- intend to cause the loss or damage; and did not fraudulently cause the loss or damage", the damages "are to be reduced to
-- the extent to which the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the claimant's share in the responsibility".
-- The share is the court's judgment: an input. Division 2A (proportionate liability among concurrent wrongdoers) and s
-- 1044B (professional standards caps) are not encoded and can reduce the amount further.
@export How much of my loss from misleading or deceptive conduct can I recover, after any reduction for my own failure to take reasonable care (Corporations Act 2001 s 1041I(1B))? The court reduces it by your share of responsibility only if the loss is economic loss or property damage, you were partly careless, and the other side neither intended the loss nor caused it fraudulently.
GIVEN `my loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of your loss or damage, in dollars
      `is it economic loss or damage to property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for economic loss or damage to property (answer no for, say, personal injury)
      `was I partly careless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the loss resulted partly from your own failure to take reasonable care (a judgment for the court)
      `did they intend it or act fraudulently` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side intended to cause the loss or caused it fraudulently
      `my share of responsibility` IS A NUMBER @desc Your share in the responsibility for the loss as the court would assess it, as a percentage from 0 to 100 (a judgment for the court)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I recover after my share of responsibility` MEANS
    IF `is it economic loss or damage to property` AND `was I partly careless` AND (NOT `did they intend it or act fraudulently`)
    THEN `my loss` TIMES (100 MINUS `my share of responsibility`) DIVIDED BY 100
    ELSE `my loss`

-- s 1041I(2): "An action under subsection (1) may be begun at any time within 6 years after the day on which the cause
-- of action arose." The cause of action arises when the loss is first suffered; that day is the person's input.
@export By what day must I start court action for loss from misleading or deceptive conduct about a financial product or service (Corporations Act 2001 s 1041I(2))? Within 6 years after the day the cause of action arose.
GIVEN `the day my cause of action arose` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose (generally the day you first suffered loss from the conduct), ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I sue under s 1041I` MEANS `the last day of 6 years after` `the day my cause of action arose`

§§ `ss 674A, 1043L, 1317HA and 1317K -- compensation orders: a listed company kept the market in the dark, and other financial services civil penalty provisions`

DECLARE `Whom I claim against` IS ONE OF
    `the listed entity`
    `a person involved in the entity's failure, such as a director or officer`

-- s 674A(2): if the listing rules require the entity to notify information as it arises, "(b) the entity has information
-- that those provisions require the entity to notify ...; (c) the information is not generally available; and (d) the
-- entity knows, or is reckless or negligent with respect to whether, the information would, if it were generally
-- available, have a material effect on the price or value of ED securities of the entity; the entity must notify the
-- market operator". (3): "A person who is involved in a listed disclosing entity's contravention of subsection (2)
-- contravenes this subsection." (4): not if the person proves they "took all steps (if any) that were reasonable" and
-- "believed on reasonable grounds" the entity was complying. s 1317E(3), the table: "subsections 674A(2), 674A(3),
-- 675A(2) and 675A(3)" are "financial services" civil penalty provisions; s 674(2) is not in the table. s 1317HA(1): "A
-- Court may order a person (the liable person) to compensate another person ... for damage suffered by the person ...
-- if: (a) the liable person has contravened a financial services civil penalty provision; and (b) the damage resulted
-- from the contravention." (2): damage includes profits made by any person resulting from the contravention. An entity
-- whose listing rules impose no such requirement is under s 675A, not encoded: NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording:
-- s 674A(1)).
@export Can I ask the court to order a listed company, or a director or officer involved, to compensate me for damage I suffered because the company did not tell the market price-sensitive information it knew about (Corporations Act 2001 ss 674A and 1317HA)? A compensation order needs the company's knowledge, recklessness or negligence about the information's effect on its share price.
GIVEN `whom do I claim against` IS A `Whom I claim against` @desc Whether you claim against the listed entity itself, or against a person involved in its failure (such as a director or officer)
      `is it listed and bound by continuous disclosure listing rules` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is listed on a market whose listing rules require it to notify information as it arises (for example a company listed on the ASX)
      `did it have information the listing rules required it to notify` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity had information that those listing rules required it to notify to the market
      `was the information generally available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information was already generally available (readily observable, or published and given time to spread among investors)
      `did it know or was it reckless or negligent about the price effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity knew, or was reckless or negligent with respect to whether, the information would have a material effect on the price or value of its securities if it were generally available
      `did it notify the market` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity notified the market operator of the information as the listing rules required
      `was the person involved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you claim against a person: whether they were involved in the entity's failure (aided it, induced it, were knowingly concerned in it or conspired in it, s 79)
      `does the person prove they took reasonable steps` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you claim against a person: whether they prove that they took all reasonable steps to make the entity comply and then believed on reasonable grounds that it was complying
      `did my damage result from the failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the damage you suffered (for example the fall in value of shares you bought while the information was hidden) resulted from the failure to disclose
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I seek compensation for a failure to disclose` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is it listed and bound by continuous disclosure listing rules`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 674A(1))
    ELSE `as an answer` (`the liable person contravened` AND `did my damage result from the failure`)
    WHERE
        `the entity contravened` MEANS
                `did it have information the listing rules required it to notify`
            AND (NOT `was the information generally available`)
            AND `did it know or was it reckless or negligent about the price effect`
            AND (NOT `did it notify the market`)
        `the liable person contravened` MEANS
            IF `whom do I claim against` EQUALS `the listed entity`
            THEN `the entity contravened`
            ELSE `the entity contravened` AND `was the person involved` AND (NOT `does the person prove they took reasonable steps`)

DECLARE `Provision contravened` IS ONE OF
    `continuous disclosure with fault (ss 674A(2), (3), 675A(2), (3))`
    `disclosing information covered by a secrecy provision (s 848C(4))`
    `conflicted remuneration, shelf-space fees, asset-based fees on borrowed amounts or avoiding Part 7.7A (ss 963E(1), (2), 963F, 963G(1), 963J, 963K(1), 963P, 964A(1), 964D(1), (2), 964E(1), 965)`
    `margin lending assessments, unsuitable facilities and margin calls (ss 985E(1), 985H(1), 985J(1), (2), (4), 985K(1), 985L, 985M(1), (2))`
    `paying loan money into an account (s 993D(4))`
    `target market determinations, distribution and stop orders (ss 994B(2), (9), 994C(2), (4), (5), (7), 994D, 994E(1), (3), 994F(1), (3) to (6), (8), 994G, 994H(3), 994J(7), (8))`
    `giving a Product Disclosure Statement (ss 1012A(5), 1012B(6), 1012C(11))`
    `superannuation product dashboards and investment information (ss 1017BA(4B), 1017BB(5AA))`
    `offers about certain managed investment schemes (s 1020A(5))`
    `defective disclosure documents, and a licensee's representatives' disclosure (ss 1021E(8), 1021G(3))`
    `product intervention orders (s 1023P(1), (2), (4))`
    `market manipulation, false trading, market rigging and spreading information about illegal transactions (ss 1041A, 1041B(1), 1041C(1), 1041D)`
    `insider trading (s 1043A(1), (2))`
    `an enforceable code provision (s 1101AC)`
    `a civil penalty provision of a regulation made under s 1101AD`
    `another provision, such as a best interests duty (ss 961K, 961L, 961Q), s 674(2) or s 1041H`

-- s 1317HA(1): "A Court may order a person (the liable person) to compensate another person ... for damage suffered by
-- the person ... if: (a) the liable person has contravened a financial services civil penalty provision; and (b) the
-- damage resulted from the contravention." s 1317E(3)(c): "a civil penalty provision categorised as a financial services
-- provision in column 3 of the following table is a financial services civil penalty provision"; (d): a provision with no
-- categorisation "is neither". The choices are every row of the table whose column 3 reads "financial services", grouped;
-- whether each was contravened is asked (only ss 674A(2), (3) are worked out, above). A provision outside them fails the
-- s 1317HA "if": JUST FALSE, worded as "this section gives no compensation order", since other routes (s 961M, s 1041I) may.
@export Can I ask the court to order someone to compensate me for damage caused by their breach of a "financial services civil penalty provision", such as the rules on product disclosure statements, target market determinations, margin lending, conflicted remuneration, market manipulation or insider trading (Corporations Act 2001 ss 1317E(3) and 1317HA)? The provision must be one the s 1317E(3) table marks as financial services, and your damage must have resulted from the breach; damage includes the profits anyone made from it. A best interests breach is not such a provision (use s 961M), nor is misleading conduct under s 1041H (use s 1041I).
GIVEN `which provision was contravened` IS A `Provision contravened` @desc Which provision the person you claim against broke, from the provisions the s 1317E(3) table marks as financial services civil penalty provisions, or another provision
      `did the person contravene it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you claim against contravened that provision (for continuous disclosure, the question about a failure to disclose works this out; for the others it is the court's finding)
      `did my damage result from the contravention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the damage you suffered (including profits anyone made from the contravention) resulted from it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I seek a compensation order for a financial services contravention` MEANS
    IF `which provision was contravened` EQUALS `another provision, such as a best interests duty (ss 961K, 961L, 961Q), s 674(2) or s 1041H`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE `as an answer` (`did the person contravene it` AND `did my damage result from the contravention`)

DECLARE `My side of the insider's trade` IS ONE OF
    `I sold the products to the insider, or to the person the insider procured`
    `I bought the products from the insider, or from the person the insider procured`

-- s 1043L(1): "If" (a) the insider possesses information not generally available that a reasonable person would expect
-- to have a material effect on the price or value of "Division 3 financial products (other than derivatives)", (b) knows
-- or is reckless as to that, and (c) in contravention of s 1043A(1) acquires or disposes of (or procures another to) any
-- such products, "the following subsections apply". (3): where the insider acquired the products "from a person ...
-- who did not possess the information, the disposer may, by action under section 1317HA, recover ... the amount (if any)
-- by which" the price at which the products were acquired from the disposer "was less than" the price at which they
-- "would have been likely to have been acquired ... if the information had been generally available". (4): the mirror for
-- an acquirer, the amount by which the disposal price "was greater than" the likely price. "The action may be taken
-- against the insider, the other person or any other person involved in the contravention." Each condition is the text's
-- "if": where one fails the amount under s 1043L is 0 (this section gives nothing on these facts; s 1317HA itself may).
-- s 1043L(7) (relief by the court) and the issuer's routes in (2), (5), (6) are not encoded.
@export How much can I recover from an insider who bought shares or other Division 3 financial products from me, or sold them to me, while holding inside information (Corporations Act 2001 ss 1043L(3), (4) and 1317HA)? The difference between the price you dealt at and the price likely if the information had been generally available, against the insider, the person they procured, or anyone else involved. 0 means s 1043L gives you nothing on these facts.
GIVEN `which side of the trade was I on` IS A `My side of the insider's trade` @desc Whether you sold the products to the insider (or to the person the insider procured to buy), or bought them from the insider (or from the person the insider procured to sell)
      `did the insider trade in contravention of s 1043A(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insider, knowing or reckless that the information was not generally available and might materially affect the price, dealt in or procured dealing in the products in contravention of s 1043A(1) (insider trading)
      `are the products derivatives` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the products were derivatives (s 1043L does not cover them)
      `did I possess the information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you yourself possessed the inside information when you dealt
      `the price I dealt at` IS A NUMBER @desc The price at which you sold or bought the products, in dollars
      `the likely price had the information been generally available` IS A NUMBER @desc The price at which the products would likely have been bought or sold at that time if the information had been generally available, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I recover from an insider under s 1043L` MEANS
    IF (NOT `did the insider trade in contravention of s 1043A(1)`) OR `are the products derivatives` OR `did I possess the information`
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `which side of the trade was I on` EQUALS `I sold the products to the insider, or to the person the insider procured`
         THEN `the shortfall of` `the price I dealt at` `below` `the likely price had the information been generally available`
         ELSE `the shortfall of` `the likely price had the information been generally available` `below` `the price I dealt at`

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
      b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the shortfall of` a `below` b MEANS IF a < b THEN b MINUS a ELSE 0

-- s 1317K: "Proceedings for a declaration of contravention, a pecuniary penalty order, or a compensation order, may be
-- started no later than 6 years after the contravention."
@export By what day must I start proceedings for a compensation order for a contravention of a financial services civil penalty provision, such as a listed company's failure to disclose (Corporations Act 2001 s 1317K)? No later than 6 years after the contravention.
GIVEN `the day of the contravention` IS A DATE @desc The day of the contravention (for a failure to disclose, the day the entity should have notified the market), ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I seek a compensation order` MEANS `the last day of 6 years after` `the day of the contravention`

§§ `Part 7.10A -- complaining to AFCA about superannuation`

DECLARE `Superannuation complaint subject` IS ONE OF
    `a fund trustee's decision about a member or beneficiary`
    `a trustee's decision to admit a member to a life policy fund`
    `an insurer's conduct in selling an annuity policy`
    `an insurer's decision under an annuity policy`
    `a superannuation provider's decision to report an amount about a person to the Commissioner of Taxation`
    `an RSA provider's conduct in opening an RSA`
    `an RSA provider's decision about an RSA holder`
    `an insurer's conduct in selling insurance paid for from an RSA`
    `an insurer's decision on insurance paid for from an RSA`
    `a death benefit decision`
    `something else`

-- s 1053(1): "a complaint made under the AFCA scheme is a superannuation complaint if the complaint is" one of (a) to (j),
-- each that a decision or conduct "is or was unfair or unreasonable". (a) and (g) need the decision to relate to "a
-- particular member, or a particular former member" (or beneficiary, or RSA holder); s 1053A takes non-member spouses
-- under Family Law Act splitting orders and agreements, and covered ADF members, to be members. (4): not a
-- superannuation complaint "to the extent that" it is about a decision of a self managed superannuation fund's trustee,
-- or an annuity policy such a trustee maintains; it reaches (a) to (e) and (j), whose decision-maker may be a self
-- managed fund's trustee or whose annuity policy such a trustee may maintain, but not the RSA complaints of (f) to (i),
-- so the answer to that question is not used for them. (5): a "decision" includes failing to decide. An "if" list and a flat
-- exclusion: JUST FALSE outside them. s 1053B: another complaint may still go to AFCA, just not as a superannuation
-- complaint.
@export Is my complaint to AFCA a "superannuation complaint", with the special powers, remedies and Federal Court appeal that brings (Corporations Act 2001 s 1053)? It must say that a superannuation trustee's, insurer's, RSA provider's or death benefit decision-maker's decision or conduct of a listed kind was unfair or unreasonable, and not be about a self managed superannuation fund.
GIVEN `what is my complaint about` IS A `Superannuation complaint subject` @desc What the complaint is about: a trustee's decision about a member or beneficiary of a regulated superannuation fund, AFCA regulated superannuation scheme or approved deposit fund; admission to a life policy fund; the sale of, or a decision under, an annuity policy; a superannuation provider's statement to the Commissioner of Taxation; opening an RSA or a decision about an RSA holder; the sale of, or a decision on, insurance paid from an RSA; a decision about paying a death benefit; or something else
      `does the decision relate to a particular member or holder` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a trustee's or RSA provider's decision: whether it relates to a particular member, former member, beneficiary or RSA holder (including a non-member spouse under a Family Law splitting order or agreement, or a covered ADF member, who are taken to be members)
      `do I say it was unfair or unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint is that the decision or conduct is or was unfair or unreasonable
      `is it about a self managed fund` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision was made by the trustee of a self managed superannuation fund, or the annuity policy is maintained by such a trustee (not used for a complaint about an RSA or insurance paid from an RSA)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my complaint a superannuation complaint` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `what is my complaint about` EQUALS `something else` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `is it about a self managed fund` AND NOT `is it an RSA complaint` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `do I say it was unfair or unreasonable`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (`what is my complaint about` EQUALS `a fund trustee's decision about a member or beneficiary` OR `what is my complaint about` EQUALS `an RSA provider's decision about an RSA holder`) AND (NOT `does the decision relate to a particular member or holder`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `is it an RSA complaint` MEANS
               `what is my complaint about` EQUALS `an RSA provider's conduct in opening an RSA`
            OR `what is my complaint about` EQUALS `an RSA provider's decision about an RSA holder`
            OR `what is my complaint about` EQUALS `an insurer's conduct in selling insurance paid for from an RSA`
            OR `what is my complaint about` EQUALS `an insurer's decision on insurance paid for from an RSA`

DECLARE `My part in the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `the complainant`
    `a person joined as a party to a superannuation complaint, not the complainant`

-- s 1051(4)(e): the scheme's determinations are "(i) binding on members of the scheme; but (ii) not binding on
-- complainants under the scheme". s 1051(2)(d): "complainants are exempt from payment of any fee or charge". s
-- 1051(4)(f): "for superannuation complaints, there are no limits on: (i) the value of claims ...; or (ii) the value of
-- remedies". AFCA must ensure these mandatory requirements are complied with (s 1052). The complainant gets the text's
-- flat "not binding": JUST FALSE. A person joined to a superannuation complaint (s 1054, s 1056A(3)) is neither a member
-- of the scheme nor named as a complainant: the text is silent, NOTHING-kind (silent: s 1051(4)(e)). The firm complained
-- about, the member of the scheme, is bound; it has no seat here.
@export Am I bound by AFCA's determination of my complaint (Corporations Act 2001 s 1051(4)(e))? Determinations bind the financial firm, a member of the scheme, but not the complainant, who may instead go to court. Complainants pay no fee (s 1051(2)(d)), and superannuation complaints have no limit on the value of the claim or the remedy (s 1051(4)(f)).
GIVEN `what is my part in the complaint` IS A `My part in the complaint` @desc Whether you made the complaint, or were joined as a party to someone else's superannuation complaint (for example a person with an interest in a death benefit)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I bound by AFCA's determination` MEANS
    IF `what is my part in the complaint` EQUALS `the complainant`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 1051(4)(e))

DECLARE `AFCA's view of the decision` IS ONE OF
    `fair and reasonable in all the circumstances`
    `unfair or unreasonable, or both`
    `AFCA has not yet decided`

DECLARE `What AFCA must or may do` IS ONE OF
    `AFCA must affirm the decision or conduct (s 1055(2), (3))`
    `AFCA may vary the decision, set it aside and substitute or remit it, or give the s 1055(6) remedies, only to remove the unfairness, and never contrary to law, the fund's rules or the policy terms (s 1055(4) to (7))`
    `nothing is decided until AFCA forms its view`

-- s 1055(2), (3): "AFCA must affirm a decision or conduct ... if AFCA is satisfied that" it "was fair and reasonable in
-- all the circumstances" (for a death benefit, in its operation in relation to the complainant and any person joined). (4),
-- (5): if satisfied it "is unfair or unreasonable, or both, AFCA may take any one or more of the actions mentioned in
-- subsection (6), but only for the purpose of placing the complainant, as nearly as practicable, in such a position that
-- the unfairness, unreasonableness, or both, no longer exists". (7): never "contrary to (a) law; or (b) ... the governing
-- rules ...; or (c) ... the terms and conditions". AFCA's satisfaction is its judgment: an input.
@export What must, or may, AFCA do with my superannuation complaint once it has formed its view of the decision or conduct (Corporations Act 2001 s 1055)?
GIVEN `what is AFCA's view` IS AN `AFCA's view of the decision` @desc Whether AFCA is satisfied that the decision (in its operation in relation to you, and anyone joined if it is a death benefit decision) or the conduct was fair and reasonable in all the circumstances, or is satisfied it was unfair or unreasonable, or has not yet decided
GIVETH A `What AFCA must or may do`
`What must AFCA do with my superannuation complaint` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what is AFCA's view`
    WHEN `fair and reasonable in all the circumstances` THEN `AFCA must affirm the decision or conduct (s 1055(2), (3))`
    WHEN `unfair or unreasonable, or both` THEN `AFCA may vary the decision, set it aside and substitute or remit it, or give the s 1055(6) remedies, only to remove the unfairness, and never contrary to law, the fund's rules or the policy terms (s 1055(4) to (7))`
    OTHERWISE `nothing is decided until AFCA forms its view`

§§§ `ss 1056 and 1056A -- death benefits (only if someone has died and a superannuation death benefit is to be paid)`

-- s 1056(1): "A person cannot make a superannuation complaint ... relating to a decision by a death benefit decision-maker
-- relating to the payment of a death benefit unless the person has an interest in the death benefit." (2): cannot if the
-- decision-maker gave the person notice of the proposed decision and of the right to object within 28 days and the person
-- "did not ... object ... within 28 days after being given notice; and" gave notice of the decision and of the right to
-- complain to AFCA within 28 days "and the person does not make the complaint to AFCA within 28 days after being given
-- notice". (3): "If a person has not been given either of the notices", the person cannot complain "unless AFCA is
-- satisfied that: (a) the person has an interest in the death benefit; and (b) it was unreasonable for the person not to
-- be given those notices". Both limbs of (2) must hold for the bar, so an objector in time is not barred by (2) even if
-- they complain late (finding 4). "Not been given either" is read as "given neither"; where only one notice was given,
-- neither (2) nor (3) bars the complaint (fork F6). Each bar is the text's own "cannot ... unless" or "cannot ... if":
-- JUST FALSE.
GIVEN `the day I acted` IS A DATE, `the day I was given that notice` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the day I acted` `is within 28 days after` `the day I was given that notice` IF
    `the day I acted` `is on or before` (`the date` 28 `days after` `the day I was given that notice`)

@export Can I complain to AFCA about a superannuation fund's decision on who gets a death benefit, given the 28-day limits for objecting and complaining (Corporations Act 2001 s 1056)?
GIVEN `do I have an interest in the death benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have an interest in the death benefit (for example you are the deceased member's spouse, child, dependant or legal personal representative and could be paid some of it)
      `was I told of the proposed decision and my right to object` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the trustee or other decision-maker gave you notice that it proposed to make the decision and that you could object within 28 days
      `the day I was told of the proposed decision` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given that notice, ISO 8601 (any date if you were not)
      `did I object` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you objected to the proposed decision by notifying the decision-maker
      `the day I objected` IS A DATE @desc The day you objected (any date if you did not)
      `was I told of the decision and my right to complain to AFCA` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker gave you notice that it had made the decision and that you could complain to AFCA within 28 days
      `the day I was told of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given that notice, ISO 8601 (any date if you were not)
      `the day I complain to AFCA` IS A DATE @desc The day you made, or will make, the complaint to AFCA
      `is AFCA satisfied I should have been told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were given neither notice: whether AFCA is satisfied that you have an interest in the death benefit and that it was unreasonable for you not to be given the notices (AFCA's judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I complain to AFCA about the death benefit decision` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `do I have an interest in the death benefit`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `was I told of the proposed decision and my right to object` AND `was I told of the decision and my right to complain to AFCA` THEN `as an answer` (`I objected in time` OR `I complained in time`)
           IF (NOT `was I told of the proposed decision and my right to object`) AND (NOT `was I told of the decision and my right to complain to AFCA`) THEN `as an answer` `is AFCA satisfied I should have been told`
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `I objected in time` MEANS `did I object` AND (`the day I objected` `is within 28 days after` `the day I was told of the proposed decision`)
        `I complained in time` MEANS `the day I complain to AFCA` `is within 28 days after` `the day I was told of the decision`

@export By what day must I object to a superannuation trustee's proposed decision on paying a death benefit, to keep my right to complain to AFCA (Corporations Act 2001 s 1056(2)(a))? Within 28 days after you were given notice of the proposed decision.
GIVEN `the day I was told of the proposed decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker gave you notice of the proposed decision and of your right to object within 28 days, ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I object to the proposed death benefit decision` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `the day I was told of the proposed decision`

@export By what day must I complain to AFCA about a death benefit decision, if I did not object to the proposed decision (Corporations Act 2001 s 1056(2)(b))? Within 28 days after you were given notice of the decision.
GIVEN `the day I was told of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker gave you notice of its decision and of your right to complain to AFCA within 28 days, ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I complain to AFCA about the death benefit decision` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `the day I was told of the decision`

-- s 1056A(1): if the decision-maker "is notified by AFCA of a superannuation complaint made in accordance with section
-- 1056 relating to the decision; the decision-maker must, within 28 days after being so notified, give notice to each
-- person (other than the complainant) whom the decision-maker believes, after reasonable enquiry, may have an interest in
-- the death benefit." The duty, from the seat of a person who may have an interest, as a regulative rule. Time is counted
-- in days from the day the decision-maker was notified by AFCA.
DECLARE `Death benefit decision-maker` IS ONE OF `the death benefit decision-maker`
DECLARE `Death benefit decision-maker's act` IS ONE OF `give me notice of the complaint to AFCA (s 1056A(1))`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Death benefit decision-maker` `Death benefit decision-maker's act`
`the decision-maker must tell me of the complaint in time` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the death benefit decision-maker`
    MUST `give me notice of the complaint to AFCA (s 1056A(1))`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the death benefit decision-maker` BECAUSE "the death benefit decision-maker did not give notice of the AFCA complaint within 28 days after AFCA notified it (s 1056A(1) of the Corporations Act 2001)"

DECLARE `What became of the duty to tell me of the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `told in time`
    `not told in time`
    `the time to tell me is still running`
    `the s 1056A duty does not reach me on these facts`

-- The trace helper, in the shape of the Public Interest Disclosures Act 2012 (Vic) encoding's. On the stable l4 a
-- CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH: where notice was given, FULFILLED means in time and
-- anything else late; where it was not, the helper asks whether giving it today would still be in time.
GIVEN `the days allowed`     IS A NUMBER
      `it has been done`     IS A BOOLEAN
      `the time it was done` IS A NUMBER
      `the time now`         IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `What became of the duty to tell me of the complaint`
`what became of the duty to tell me` `the days allowed` `it has been done` `the time it was done` `the time now` MEANS
    IF `it has been done`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the decision-maker must tell me of the complaint in time` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time it was done`), (EVENT `the death benefit decision-maker` `give me notice of the complaint to AFCA (s 1056A(1))` `the time it was done`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `told in time`
         OTHERWISE `not told in time`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the decision-maker must tell me of the complaint in time` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time now`), (EVENT `the death benefit decision-maker` `give me notice of the complaint to AFCA (s 1056A(1))` `the time now`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the time to tell me is still running`
         OTHERWISE `not told in time`

@export Someone has complained to AFCA about a superannuation death benefit decision: was I, as a person who may have an interest in the benefit, told of the complaint in time (Corporations Act 2001 s 1056A(1))? The decision-maker must notify each person it believes, after reasonable enquiry, may have an interest, within 28 days after AFCA notifies it.
GIVEN `am I the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who made the complaint (the duty is to tell the others)
      `does the decision-maker believe I may have an interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the trustee or other decision-maker believes, after reasonable enquiry, that you may have an interest in the death benefit (answer yes if reasonable enquiry would show it)
      `the day AFCA notified the decision-maker` IS A DATE @desc The day AFCA notified the decision-maker of the complaint, ISO 8601
      `have I been told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker has given you notice of the complaint
      `the day I was told` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given that notice (any date if you have not been)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, ISO 8601
GIVETH A `What became of the duty to tell me of the complaint`
`Was I told of the death benefit complaint in time` MEANS
    IF `am I the complainant` OR (NOT `does the decision-maker believe I may have an interest`)
    THEN `the s 1056A duty does not reach me on these facts`
    ELSE `what became of the duty to tell me` 28 `have I been told` (`the days from` `the day AFCA notified the decision-maker` `to` `the day I was told`) (`the days from` `the day AFCA notified the decision-maker` `to` `today`)

-- s 1056A(2)(c): the notice must "state that the person may apply to be joined as a party to the complaint by giving
-- notice to AFCA within 28 days after the person is given notice, or such longer period as AFCA allows". (3): otherwise
-- joined only if AFCA is satisfied the person should be.
@export By what day must I apply to AFCA to be joined as a party to a complaint about a superannuation death benefit (Corporations Act 2001 s 1056A(2)(c))? Within 28 days after you were given notice of the complaint, or any longer period AFCA allows.
GIVEN `the day I was told of the complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker gave you notice of the complaint, ISO 8601
      `extra days AFCA allows` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days beyond the 28 AFCA has allowed you (0 if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to be joined` MEANS `the date` (28 PLUS `extra days AFCA allows`) `days after` `the day I was told of the complaint`

§§§ `s 1057 -- appealing AFCA's superannuation determination`

-- s 1057(1): "A party to a superannuation complaint may appeal to the Federal Court, on a question of law, from AFCA's
-- determination". (2)(a): "not later than the 28th day after the day on which a copy of the determination of AFCA is
-- given to the person, or within such further period as the Federal Court (whether before or after the end of that day)
-- allows". A grant limited to parties and to questions of law: outside it NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s
-- 1057(1)). The period is the text's own: late, JUST FALSE. (5): a complainant who does not defend another party's appeal
-- cannot be ordered to pay costs.
@export Can I still appeal AFCA's determination of my superannuation complaint to the Federal Court (Corporations Act 2001 s 1057)? Only a party, only on a question of law, and no later than the 28th day after you were given a copy of the determination, unless the Court allows more time.
GIVEN `am I a party to the complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the superannuation complaint (the complainant, or a person AFCA joined)
      `is my appeal on a question of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your appeal raises a question of law (not just a disagreement with AFCA's view of the facts)
      `the day I was given a copy of the determination` IS A DATE @desc The day a copy of AFCA's determination was given to you, ISO 8601
      `the day I file the appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you institute (or will institute) the appeal in the Federal Court, ISO 8601
      `has the Federal Court allowed more time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Federal Court has allowed you a further period to appeal
      `the last day of the further period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of any further period the Court allowed (any date if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal AFCA's superannuation determination` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`am I a party to the complaint` AND `is my appeal on a question of law`))
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 1057(1))
    ELSE `as an answer` (   (`the day I file the appeal` `is on or before` (`the date` 28 `days after` `the day I was given a copy of the determination`))
                         OR (`has the Federal Court allowed more time` AND (`the day I file the appeal` `is on or before` `the last day of the further period`)))

§§ `Part 9.4AAA -- whistleblowers (only if you have reported, or are thinking of reporting, misconduct)`

DECLARE `Kind of entity` IS ONE OF
    `a company`
    `a trading or financial corporation`
    `an ADI, its authorised NOHC or a subsidiary of either`
    `a general insurer, its authorised NOHC or a subsidiary of either`
    `a life company, its registered NOHC or a subsidiary of either`
    `a superannuation entity or its trustee`
    `an entity prescribed by the regulations`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `My connection with the entity` IS ONE OF
    `an officer`
    `an employee`
    `I supply services or goods to it, paid or unpaid`
    `an employee of someone who supplies services or goods to it`
    `an associate of the entity`
    `a trustee, custodian or investment manager of the superannuation entity, or an officer, employee or supplier of one`
    `a relative of a person in one of those groups`
    `a dependant of a person in one of those groups, or of their spouse`
    `an individual the regulations prescribe for the entity`
    `no such connection`

-- s 1317AAB: "Each of the following is a regulated entity" (a) to (g): a list without "only", so an entity outside it is
-- NOTHING-kind (open list: s 1317AAB). s 1317AAA: "An individual is an eligible whistleblower in relation to a regulated
-- entity if the individual is, or has been, any of the following" (a) to (i); (f) is for "a regulated entity that is a
-- superannuation entity". An "if": JUST FALSE outside it.
@export Am I an "eligible whistleblower" for this company, bank, insurer or superannuation fund, so that my disclosures about it can be protected (Corporations Act 2001 ss 1317AAA and 1317AAB)? Current and former officers, employees, suppliers and their employees, associates, and the relatives and dependants of any of them qualify.
GIVEN `what kind of entity is it` IS A `Kind of entity` @desc What the entity you are reporting on is: a company, a trading or financial corporation, a bank or other ADI (or its holding company or a subsidiary), a general insurer, a life company, a superannuation fund or its trustee, an entity prescribed by the regulations, or none of these (for example a government department or a partnership)
      `what is my connection with it` IS A `My connection with the entity` @desc Your connection with the entity, now or in the past: officer, employee, supplier of services or goods (paid or unpaid), employee of a supplier, associate, for a superannuation fund its trustee, custodian or investment manager (or their officers, employees or suppliers), a relative or dependant of any of those, a person prescribed by the regulations, or no such connection
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I an eligible whistleblower` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `none of these` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (open list: s 1317AAB)
           IF `what is my connection with it` EQUALS `no such connection` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `what is my connection with it` EQUALS `a trustee, custodian or investment manager of the superannuation entity, or an officer, employee or supplier of one` THEN `as an answer` (`what kind of entity is it` EQUALS `a superannuation entity or its trustee`)
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `Whom I disclosed to` IS ONE OF
    `ASIC`
    `APRA`
    `a Commonwealth authority prescribed for the entity`
    `an eligible recipient of the entity`
    `a legal practitioner, for advice or representation about the whistleblower protections`
    `a member of an Australian parliament or a journalist`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Further disclosure kind` IS ONE OF
    `a public interest disclosure`
    `an emergency disclosure`

DECLARE `Protection route` IS ONE OF
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority (s 1317AA(1))`
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to an eligible recipient (s 1317AA(2))`
    `it qualifies: a disclosure to a lawyer (s 1317AA(3))`
    `it qualifies: a public interest disclosure (s 1317AAD(1))`
    `it qualifies: an emergency disclosure (s 1317AAD(2))`
    `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`

-- s 1317AAD(1)(b): "at least 90 days have passed since the previous disclosure was made"; (e) "after the end of the
-- period referred to in paragraph (b), the discloser gave the body ... a written notification". The 90 days start the
-- day after the disclosure and have passed at the start of the 91st day after it (fork F7).
GIVEN `the day of my earlier disclosure` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day after the 90 days have passed since` `the day of my earlier disclosure` MEANS `the date` 91 `days after` `the day of my earlier disclosure`

@export What is the earliest day on which I can make a public interest disclosure to a journalist or member of parliament, after my disclosure to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority (Corporations Act 2001 s 1317AAD(1)(b), (e))? At least 90 days must have passed since that disclosure, and only then may you give that body the written notice of your intention, before going public.
GIVEN `the day of my earlier disclosure` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the earlier disclosure to ASIC, APRA or the prescribed authority, ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`When is the earliest I can make a public interest disclosure` MEANS `the first day after the 90 days have passed since` `the day of my earlier disclosure`

-- s 1317AA(1): to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed Commonwealth authority, by an eligible whistleblower, where (4) or (5)
-- applies: (4) "the discloser has reasonable grounds to suspect that the information concerns misconduct, or an improper
-- state of affairs or circumstances" in relation to the entity or a related body corporate; (5) reasonable grounds to
-- suspect a contravention of the listed financial Acts, a Commonwealth offence punishable by 12 months or more, a danger
-- to the public or the financial system, or prescribed conduct. (2): to an eligible recipient (s 1317AAC: an officer,
-- senior manager, auditor, actuary or authorised person of the entity or a related body corporate; for a superannuation
-- entity, also a trustee or a director of a trustee). (3): to a legal practitioner "for the purpose of obtaining legal
-- advice or legal representation in relation to the operation of this Part" -- no other condition. s 1317AADA: (1) and
-- (2) "do not apply" to the extent the information concerns a personal work-related grievance of the discloser (about
-- their employment, with personal implications, without significant implications for the entity beyond them, and not
-- about (5)(c) to (f) conduct) and not detriment or a threat to them in breach of s 1317AC. s 1317AAD(1), (2): to a
-- member of parliament or a journalist, after a previous disclosure of the information that qualified under s 1317AA(1):
-- for a public interest disclosure, 90 days passed, no reasonable grounds to believe action is being or has been taken,
-- reasonable grounds to believe it is in the public interest, a written notification after the 90 days identifying the
-- earlier disclosure and stating the intention, and no more disclosed than necessary; for an emergency disclosure,
-- reasonable grounds to believe the information concerns "a substantial and imminent danger to the health or safety of
-- one or more persons or to the natural environment", the written notification, and no more than necessary. Each is an
-- "if": a disclosure meeting none of them does not qualify (the Part's protection is the text's own no; other law may
-- still protect it).
@export Does my disclosure of misconduct qualify for the whistleblower protections of the Corporations Act 2001 (Part 9.4AAA, ss 1317AA, 1317AAC, 1317AAD and 1317AADA)? Disclosures to ASIC, APRA, a prescribed authority or an eligible recipient inside the entity qualify when you have reasonable grounds to suspect misconduct (but not for a purely personal work-related grievance); disclosures to a lawyer about the protections always qualify; disclosures to a journalist or member of parliament qualify only as a public interest or emergency disclosure after an earlier qualifying disclosure to a regulator and a written warning to it.
GIVEN `am I an eligible whistleblower` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an eligible whistleblower for the entity (the answer to the eligible whistleblower question)
      `whom did I disclose to` IS A `Whom I disclosed to` @desc To whom you made the disclosure: ASIC; APRA; a Commonwealth authority prescribed for the entity; an eligible recipient of the entity (an officer, senior manager, auditor, member of an audit team, actuary or person authorised to receive disclosures, of the entity or a related company; for a superannuation fund also a trustee or a director of the trustee); a lawyer, to get advice or representation about these protections; a member of an Australian parliament or a journalist; or someone else
      `do I have reasonable grounds to suspect misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to suspect that the information concerns misconduct or an improper state of affairs at the entity (or a related company), or a breach of the Corporations Act, ASIC Act, Banking, Insurance, Life Insurance, Credit or Superannuation Acts, a Commonwealth offence punishable by 12 months' prison or more, or a danger to the public or the financial system
      `is it only a personal work-related grievance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information concerns only a grievance about your own employment (such as a conflict with a colleague, a transfer, promotion, pay or discipline decision) with implications for you personally, has no significant implications for the entity beyond you, and does not concern a breach of the listed laws, a 12-month offence or a danger
      `is it about victimisation of me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information concerns detriment caused to you, or a threat made to you, because of a whistleblower disclosure (victimisation under s 1317AC)
      `what kind of further disclosure was it` IS A `Further disclosure kind` @desc For a disclosure to a journalist or member of parliament: whether it was a public interest disclosure or an emergency disclosure
      `did an earlier disclosure qualify under s 1317AA(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a journalist or member of parliament: whether you earlier disclosed the same information to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority in a way that qualified for protection
      `the day of my earlier disclosure` IS A DATE @desc The day of that earlier disclosure, ISO 8601 (any date if none)
      `did I give that body written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you then gave that body a written notice identifying the earlier disclosure and saying you intend to make a public interest (or emergency) disclosure
      `the day I gave written notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave that written notice (any date if none)
      `the day of my further disclosure` IS A DATE @desc The day you disclosed to the journalist or member of parliament (any date if not applicable)
      `do I have reasonable grounds to believe action is being taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a public interest disclosure: whether you have reasonable grounds to believe that action is being, or has been, taken to address the matters in your earlier disclosure
      `do I have reasonable grounds to believe it is in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a public interest disclosure: whether you have reasonable grounds to believe that disclosing further would be in the public interest
      `do I have reasonable grounds to believe there is an imminent danger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an emergency disclosure: whether you have reasonable grounds to believe the information concerns a substantial and imminent danger to the health or safety of one or more people or to the natural environment
      `did I disclose no more than necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a journalist or member of parliament: whether you disclosed no more than was necessary to inform them of the misconduct (or of the danger)
GIVETH A `Protection route`
`Does my disclosure qualify for protection` MEANS
    CONSIDER `whom did I disclose to`
    WHEN `a legal practitioner, for advice or representation about the whistleblower protections` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to a lawyer (s 1317AA(3))`
    WHEN `ASIC` THEN IF `the s 1317AA conditions hold` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority (s 1317AA(1))` ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    WHEN `APRA` THEN IF `the s 1317AA conditions hold` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority (s 1317AA(1))` ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    WHEN `a Commonwealth authority prescribed for the entity` THEN IF `the s 1317AA conditions hold` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority (s 1317AA(1))` ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    WHEN `an eligible recipient of the entity` THEN IF `the s 1317AA conditions hold` THEN `it qualifies: a disclosure to an eligible recipient (s 1317AA(2))` ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    WHEN `a member of an Australian parliament or a journalist` THEN
        IF `what kind of further disclosure was it` EQUALS `a public interest disclosure`
        THEN IF `the s 1317AAD(1) conditions hold` THEN `it qualifies: a public interest disclosure (s 1317AAD(1))` ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
        ELSE IF `the s 1317AAD(2) conditions hold` THEN `it qualifies: an emergency disclosure (s 1317AAD(2))` ELSE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    OTHERWISE `it does not qualify for protection on these facts`
    WHERE
        `the grievance carve-out applies` MEANS `is it only a personal work-related grievance` AND (NOT `is it about victimisation of me`)
        `the s 1317AA conditions hold` MEANS
                `am I an eligible whistleblower`
            AND `do I have reasonable grounds to suspect misconduct`
            AND (NOT `the grievance carve-out applies`)
        `the common further conditions hold` MEANS
                `did an earlier disclosure qualify under s 1317AA(1)`
            AND `did I give that body written notice`
            AND (`the day I gave written notice` `is on or before` `the day of my further disclosure`)
            AND `did I disclose no more than necessary`
        `the s 1317AAD(1) conditions hold` MEANS
                `the common further conditions hold`
            AND (`the day I gave written notice` `is on or after` (`the first day after the 90 days have passed since` `the day of my earlier disclosure`))
            AND (NOT `do I have reasonable grounds to believe action is being taken`)
            AND `do I have reasonable grounds to believe it is in the public interest`
        `the s 1317AAD(2) conditions hold` MEANS
                `the common further conditions hold`
            AND `do I have reasonable grounds to believe there is an imminent danger`

-- s 1317AB(1): "If a person makes a disclosure that qualifies for protection under this Part: (a) the person is not
-- subject to any civil, criminal or administrative liability (including disciplinary action) for making the disclosure;
-- and (b) no contractual or other remedy may be enforced, and no contractual or other right may be exercised, against
-- the person on the basis of the disclosure". (2): qualified privilege, and no termination of a contract on the basis
-- that the disclosure breaches it. A flat negative: the immunity is JUST TRUE. The Note: no immunity for the person's
-- own conduct that the disclosure reveals. For a disclosure that does not qualify the Part is silent: NOTHING-kind
-- (silent: s 1317AB reaches only qualifying disclosures).
@export Am I protected from being sued, prosecuted, disciplined or having my contract ended for making my disclosure (Corporations Act 2001 s 1317AB)? Yes, for a disclosure that qualifies for protection, though not for your own misconduct that it reveals.
GIVEN `does my disclosure qualify` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your disclosure qualifies for protection (the answer to the qualifying disclosure question)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I protected from liability for my disclosure` MEANS
    IF `does my disclosure qualify` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 1317AB reaches only qualifying disclosures)

-- s 1317AB(1)(c): "if the disclosure qualifies for protection under this Part under subsection 1317AA(1) or section
-- 1317AAD--the information is not admissible in evidence against the person in criminal proceedings or in proceedings for
-- the imposition of a penalty, other than proceedings in respect of the falsity of the information." For the other routes
-- the section says nothing: NOTHING-kind (silent: s 1317AB(1)(c) covers only s 1317AA(1) and s 1317AAD).
@export Is the information I disclosed kept out of evidence against me in criminal or penalty proceedings (Corporations Act 2001 s 1317AB(1)(c))? Only for disclosures to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority and public interest or emergency disclosures, and not in proceedings about whether the information was false.
GIVEN `how did my disclosure qualify` IS A `Protection route` @desc How your disclosure qualified for protection (the answer to the qualifying disclosure question)
      `are the proceedings about whether it was false` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are about the falsity of the information you disclosed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my disclosure kept out of evidence against me` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `how did my disclosure qualify` EQUALS `it qualifies: a disclosure to ASIC, APRA or a prescribed authority (s 1317AA(1))` OR `how did my disclosure qualify` EQUALS `it qualifies: a public interest disclosure (s 1317AAD(1))` OR `how did my disclosure qualify` EQUALS `it qualifies: an emergency disclosure (s 1317AAD(2))` THEN `as an answer` (NOT `are the proceedings about whether it was false`)
           OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 1317AB(1)(c) covers only s 1317AA(1) and s 1317AAD)

DECLARE `What was revealed` IS ONE OF
    `my identity`
    `information likely to lead to my identification`
    `nothing that identifies me`

DECLARE `Whom it was revealed to` IS ONE OF
    `ASIC, APRA or a member of the AFP`
    `a legal practitioner, for advice or representation about the whistleblower protections`
    `a person or body prescribed by the regulations`
    `anyone else`

-- s 1317AAE(1): a person contravenes if the discloser made a qualifying disclosure, the person discloses "(i) the
-- identity of the discloser; (ii) information that is likely to lead to the identification of the discloser", obtained
-- "directly or indirectly because of the qualifying disclosure", and the disclosure "is not authorised under subsection
-- (2) or (3)". (2): authorised if made to ASIC, APRA, an AFP member, a legal practitioner for advice about the Part, a
-- prescribed person or body, or "with the consent of the discloser". (3): authorised if made by ASIC, APRA or the AFP to a
-- Commonwealth, State or Territory authority or State body to help it perform its functions. (4): "Subsection (1) does
-- not apply if" what was disclosed is not the identity, is reasonably necessary to investigate, and all reasonable steps
-- were taken to reduce the risk of identification. Conditions and a flat exception: JUST FALSE where they fail.
@export Did someone break the law by revealing my identity as a whistleblower, or information likely to identify me (Corporations Act 2001 s 1317AAE)? It is an offence and a civil penalty provision unless the disclosure was to ASIC, APRA, the AFP, a lawyer or a prescribed body, was with your consent, was a regulator passing it on, or (for information other than your identity) was reasonably necessary to investigate with all reasonable steps taken to protect you.
GIVEN `does my disclosure qualify` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your disclosure qualified for protection
      `what did they reveal` IS A `What was revealed` @desc What the person revealed: your identity, information likely to lead to your identification, or nothing that identifies you
      `did they get it because of my disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person obtained that information, directly or indirectly, because of your disclosure
      `to whom did they reveal it` IS A `Whom it was revealed to` @desc To whom it was revealed: ASIC, APRA or a member of the Australian Federal Police; a lawyer, for advice or representation about the whistleblower protections; a person or body prescribed by the regulations; or anyone else
      `did I consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consented to it being revealed
      `was it a regulator passing it to another authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was ASIC, APRA or the AFP passing it to a Commonwealth, State or Territory authority (or a State or Territory statutory body) to help that body perform its functions
      `was it reasonably necessary to investigate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether revealing it was reasonably necessary to investigate the matters you disclosed
      `did they take all reasonable steps to protect me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person took all reasonable steps to reduce the risk that you would be identified
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was revealing my identity unlawful` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `does my disclosure qualify`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `what did they reveal` EQUALS `nothing that identifies me` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `did they get it because of my disclosure`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT (`to whom did they reveal it` EQUALS `anyone else`)) OR `did I consent` OR `was it a regulator passing it to another authority` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `what did they reveal` EQUALS `information likely to lead to my identification` AND `was it reasonably necessary to investigate` AND `did they take all reasonable steps to protect me` THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `Whom I seek the order against` IS ONE OF
    `the person who harmed or threatened me`
    `an officer or employee involved in their company's conduct`
    `a company that had a duty to prevent another person's conduct`

-- s 1317AD(1): the court may make an order if the first person's conduct "(i) causes any detriment to another person
-- (the second person); or (ii) constitutes the making of a threat to cause any such detriment", "when the first person
-- engages in the detrimental conduct, the first person believes or suspects that the second person or any other person
-- made, may have made, proposes to make or could make a disclosure that qualifies for protection", and "the belief or
-- suspicion ... is the reason, or part of the reason". (2): an officer or employee of a body corporate that did so, who
-- aided, abetted, counselled or procured, induced, was knowingly concerned in or conspired in it. (2A): a body corporate
-- under a duty to prevent, or to take reasonable steps against, another person's detrimental conduct that fails in part
-- or whole. (2B): the person seeking the order need only point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of the
-- detriment (and, for (2A), the duty); the other side must then prove the claim is not made out. s 1317ADA: detriment
-- "includes (without limitation)" dismissal, injury, adverse change of duties, discrimination, harassment or
-- intimidation, harm including psychological harm, damage to property, reputation, business or financial position, and
-- "any other damage". The court's power is "may"; the answer is whether the court may make an order.
@export Can a court order compensation or other remedies against a person who harmed me, or threatened to, because they believed or suspected I had made or might make a whistleblower disclosure (Corporations Act 2001 ss 1317AD and 1317ADA)? Officers and employees involved, and a company that failed in a duty to prevent it, can also be ordered. You need only point to evidence of a reasonable possibility of the detriment; the other side must then disprove the claim.
GIVEN `whom do I seek the order against` IS A `Whom I seek the order against` @desc Whether you seek the order against the person (or company) that harmed or threatened you, an officer or employee involved in their company's conduct, or a company that had a duty to prevent another person's conduct
      `was I harmed or threatened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct caused you detriment (for example dismissal, injury, a worse position or duties, discrimination, harassment, psychological harm, damage to your property, reputation, business or finances, or any other damage) or was a threat to cause detriment to you
      `did they believe or suspect a disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when they acted, the person who harmed or threatened you believed or suspected that you or someone else made, may have made, proposed to make or could make a qualifying disclosure
      `was that belief part of the reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that belief or suspicion was the reason, or part of the reason, for the conduct
      `was the officer or employee involved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an officer or employee: whether they aided, abetted, counselled or procured, induced, were knowingly concerned in, or conspired in the company's conduct
      `did the company have a duty to prevent it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a company with a duty: whether it was under a duty to prevent the other person's conduct, or to take reasonable steps to ensure it did not happen
      `did the company fail in that duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a company with a duty: whether it failed, wholly or partly, to fulfil that duty
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court order a remedy for the harm I suffered` MEANS
    CONSIDER `whom do I seek the order against`
    WHEN `the person who harmed or threatened me` THEN `as an answer` `the s 1317AD(1) conditions hold`
    WHEN `an officer or employee involved in their company's conduct` THEN `as an answer` (`the s 1317AD(1) conditions hold` AND `was the officer or employee involved`)
    WHEN `a company that had a duty to prevent another person's conduct` THEN `as an answer` (`the s 1317AD(1) conditions hold` AND `did the company have a duty to prevent it` AND `did the company fail in that duty`)
    WHERE
        `the s 1317AD(1) conditions hold` MEANS `was I harmed or threatened` AND `did they believe or suspect a disclosure` AND `was that belief part of the reason`

DECLARE `Whistleblower remedy` IS ONE OF
    `compensation from the person for my loss, damage or injury`
    `compensation from the person's employer, in part, jointly with the person, or alone`
    `an injunction to prevent, stop or remedy the effects of the conduct`
    `an apology`
    `reinstatement in my position or a comparable one`
    `exemplary damages`
    `any other order the court thinks appropriate`

-- s 1317AE(1): the court may order (a) compensation by the first person; (b) "if the court is satisfied that the first
-- person engaged in the detrimental conduct in connection with the first person's position as an employee", compensation
-- by the employer in part, jointly, or alone; (c) an injunction; (d) an apology; (e) "if the second person is or was
-- employed in a particular position and the detrimental conduct wholly or partly consists ... of the termination, or
-- purported termination, of the second person's employment", reinstatement; (f) "if the court thinks it is appropriate",
-- exemplary damages; (g) "any other order the court thinks appropriate". (2): where the conduct ended the person's
-- employment (including by forcing a resignation), the court must consider how long the person is likely to be without
-- work. (3): for an employer order the court may consider its precautions, its whistleblower policy and its duties.
@export What orders can the court make for me against the person who harmed me for being a whistleblower (Corporations Act 2001 s 1317AE)?
GIVEN `can the court order a remedy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court may make an order (the answer to the question whether the court can order a remedy for the harm)
      `did they act in their position as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the person engaged in the conduct in connection with their position as an employee (so their employer can be ordered to pay too)
      `did the conduct end my employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was or included the termination, or purported termination, of your employment in a particular position
GIVETH A LIST OF `Whistleblower remedy`
`What orders can the court make for me` MEANS
    IF (NOT `can the court order a remedy`)
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE `compensation from the person for my loss, damage or injury` FOLLOWED BY (IF `did they act in their position as an employee` THEN `compensation from the person's employer, in part, jointly with the person, or alone` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` ELSE `the rest`)
    WHERE
        `the tail` MEANS LIST `exemplary damages`, `any other order the court thinks appropriate`
        `the rest` MEANS
            `an injunction to prevent, stop or remedy the effects of the conduct` FOLLOWED BY (`an apology` FOLLOWED BY (IF `did the conduct end my employment` THEN `reinstatement in my position or a comparable one` FOLLOWED BY `the tail` ELSE `the tail`))

-- s 1317AH(2): "The claimant must not be ordered by the court to pay costs incurred by another party to the proceedings,
-- except in accordance with subsection (3)". (3): "only if" the court is satisfied the claimant instituted the proceedings
-- "vexatiously or without reasonable cause", or that "the claimant's unreasonable act or omission caused the other party
-- to incur the costs". An "only if": JUST FALSE outside it.
@export Can I be ordered to pay the other side's legal costs if I go to court for compensation as a whistleblower (Corporations Act 2001 s 1317AH)? Only if you started the case vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or your unreasonable act or omission caused them the costs.
GIVEN `did I sue vexatiously or without reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that you instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause
      `did my unreasonable act cause their costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that an unreasonable act or omission of yours caused the other party to incur the costs
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be ordered to pay the other side's costs` MEANS
    `as an answer` (`did I sue vexatiously or without reasonable cause` OR `did my unreasonable act cause their costs`)
